  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIsolated showers and thunderstorms will wind down as a cold \nfront exits the area this afternoon. Another round of isolated\nshowers may develop in the southeastern half of the forecast \narea as a shortwave passes by late tonight into Monday morning.\nDrier conditions then take hold as high pressure builds in at\nthe surface Monday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOn Monday night, winds calm and skies clear under the influence of \nhigh pressure. This scenario should facilitate good radiational \ncooling and send temperatures down into the 30s to 40s, with \npotential for frost and freeze conditions to develop across \nportions of the mountains, northern WV, and southeast OH early \nTuesday morning. A Freeze Watch has already been issued for \nSoutheast Randolph and Northwest Pocahontas Counties where \nconfidence in sub-freezing temperatures is highest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nA low pressure system traveling through southern Canada reintroduces \nchances of rain and thunderstorms as it steers a cold front through \nthe area mid week. A few stronger storms could be possible as\ninstability increases during the afternoon. Models soundings\ncurrently project the best environment for stronger storms \nwould be in the western third of the forecast area, with less \nfavorable conditions to the east. Precipitation coverage is \nexpected to taper off behind the front, though additional \nshowers may develop in and near the mountains as an upper trough\npasses over the area Wednesday night into Thursday. High \npressure then regains control for the end of the work week.",
